\f1\fs22 Johnny regarded the booth with the same distaste he might reserve for an outhouse toilet. He dusted the seat with a paper napkin and dropped his prodigious butt on it, too near Sarah for her comfort. \'93Did you get a look at the heads out there? It\'92s like a leper\'92s grog den. Haven\'92t they heard of cosmetic surgery?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah rolled her eyes and shook her head.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And what is that smell? My God, I am going to have to burn these clothes when I get home.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You always throw your clothes away when you get home,\'94 Sarah said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny nodded the truth of it. What good was an endowment if you wore the same clothes twice? \'93Are you planning to drink me under the table again? Because, let me tell you, I preloaded this time.\'94 He exhaled; Sarah smelled Argentinian brandy, the obscenely expensive kind.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah lifted a napkin she\'92d covered the disc with. \'93Any idea what this is?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny peered down his nose at it. \'93I dunno. You cut a hole in one of those appalling jackets they wear here?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s not leather,\'94 Sarah said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny was mid-sneer when a rainbow shimmer danced over the disc. For half a second, he was transfixed, then faked nonchalance, even as his fingers twitched to poke at it. \'93Weird light tricks. Probably Cloudborne,\'94 he muttered, then made a half-hearted grab that was more about accidental groping than scientific method.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah brushed him away and slammed her hand over the disc.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Taxing whore claws!\'94 Johnny swore, jabbering something unintelligible, then said, \'93Did you see it flash?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah grimaced. \'93No?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny shunted his lower jaw forward while he thought. \'93Where did you get this?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A friend crocheted it for me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny\'92s face etched grim. \'93Are you in trouble?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah couldn\'92t bluff that away. She felt her anxiety lighting up every nerve in her body.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A PAPER agent knows. Well, actually it\'92s more he believes I have it; he doesn\'92t know for sure\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You have PAPER on your arse?\'94 Johnny\'92s pitch ascended to the chandeliers, as reptilian parts of his brain contemplated his mode of demise.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, that\'92s an embarrassing thing to accuse a lady of.\'94 Sarah\'92s joke withered in her throat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny\'92s usually ruddy complexion leeched grey, as if she had burned his favourite Obi Wan figurine. \'93Sarah, you do know how to pick your enemies.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He clutched his tablet like a life raft and vocalised at it with a series of security clicks, chirps, and whistles, then fetched a black-and-white picture of his grandmother from his suit coat and presented it to its cameras. After it binged, he began thumbing furiously for facts.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah waited patiently until Johnny\'92s face sank. \'93Nothing?\'94 she guessed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93All I can find is some ancient drawing,\'94 Johnny grumbled, showing her a grainy image from a site that looked three centuries out of date. \'93Apparently circles have always freaked people out. Could be Atlantis, Lemuria, 1980s government health logo\'97hard to tell.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah nearly laughed, except her nerves were now jumpy for real. \'93Johnny, seriously. Is it dangerous?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny straightened, catching her anxiety and trying to weaponise it into confidence. \'93Sarah, you know me. If it is dangerous, I\'92ll take care of it. I\'92ve handled worse.\'94 A beat. \'93Actually, that\'92s not true, but I can go harder than anyone you know when I\'92m researching.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah snorted. \'93Just as long as you don\'92t try to toss it,\'94 she said carelessly. \'93Or lick it,\'94 she added too quickly, then sat back and tried to look casual.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny seized the disc.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Johnny, no! Give it back!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Did you toss it, Sarah? Or lick it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s blood chilled. \'93Ah\'85 lick it? No\'85 please, just give it back.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Her pleas alarmed Johnny. \'93Then what?\'94 Johnny was maniacally insistent.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s gaze narrowed on the disc clasped between Johnny\'92s knuckles.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Okay, well then.\'94 Johnny reassumed his sense of superiority. \'93I propose an experiment,\'94 he said, and balanced the disc on his thumb as if he would toss it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Johnny, no!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The room fell silent; hostile eyes drifted toward the booth where a pair of tourists were apparently having a tiff.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Don\'92t toss it. Please, Johnny.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny cradled the disc in his baby-soft hand. \'93Go on.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I already tossed it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Even Johnny could infer that was significant. \'93Something happened,\'94 he guessed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, something happened, I guess, after. But, it must have been coincidence, right?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What. Happened.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah hesitated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93C\'92mon, Sarah, what?\'94 Then Johnny started mocking. \'93Time jumped? Did you feel the earth move?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Look, Johnny, I\'92m not going to fuck you just because you\'92re scaring me.\'94 But her face was ashen. \'93Just, please, just give me back the disc. Let\'92s forget about it, have a few drinks and a laugh, yeah?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Wary, Johnny went to put the disc in his pocket.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Give it back,\'94 Sarah ordered, in a panic.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Slowly, Johnny placed the disc back on the table where Sarah would have to reach across him for it. \'93Look, Sarah. You asked for my help.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s shoulders sagged. \'93I just want information, Johnny. You\'92re usually so eager to please, you sprout all this knowledge, eager to impress me and it\'92s all about what you know and you never ask even a single thing about me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny released a long, slow breath. His eyes glazed over as he considered that Sarah might have no feelings for him whatsoever. \'93I can\'92t protect you if you don\'92t tell me what\'92s going on.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah let her presumed need to be protected slip through to the keeper. \'93Well, after I tossed the disc, we had a car crash,\'94 she admitted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I knew you tossed it!\'94 Johnny said, elated. \'93I just knew it!\'94 Then: \'93Wait. We?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shot a look over at Chanta. Suspicious, Johnny followed the trace of her gaze, but saw nothing or no one remarkable. Just an angry-looking guy with a burned face and frazzled hair.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Never mind,\'94 Sarah said. \'93A van I was in\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Van? As in, a ground vehicle? What the hell would you be doing travelling down here?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We hit nothing, but still the van flipped over on its side, and we slid into a garlic sauce storm.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Will you shut up? If I\'92m fucking someone, I promise you\'92ll be the third to know. Just tell me, did this disc cause the van to crash?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While Sarah was getting much too familiar with The Johnny, Chanta rearranged a pyramid of empty bottles on a rickety table. He ground his teeth, and hurled ninja stars with his eyes at The Johnny, who was looking crazier all the time. But crazy is a relative term, which became apparent when three stick-insect men with long white beards approached Sarah\'92s booth, wearing the kind of hooded onesies wizards might wear to bed.
